00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
YOUTUBE REVENUE:
YouTube’s revenue is declining for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:10.120
time in history. For 3 straight quarters between&nbsp;
the middle of 2022 and the first quarter of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:16.600
Google saw overall YouTube ad revenue decline.&nbsp;
To their credit, YouTube ad revenue did start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:25.040
growing again in Q2 of 2023 and we may see another&nbsp;
positive in Q3 but whether Q3 is positive or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.760
the larger concern still remains: has YouTube ad&nbsp;
revenue reached it’s peak. Take a look at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:37.320
graph for example. Between 2017 and 2021,&nbsp;
YouTube ad revenue nearly quadrupled. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:45.040
from 2021 to 2022, ad revenue stayed basically the&nbsp;
same. And with 2023 revenue estimates coming at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.640
just over $30 billion, it looks like YouTube is in&nbsp;
for another year of near zero revenue growth. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.760
of Wall Street has written off this decline as&nbsp;
just YouTube maturing and the ad market softening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:03.200
due to high inflation and recession fears. But,&nbsp;
I think there’s actually a much more interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.360
factor at play: YouTube shorts. Eversince short&nbsp;
form content started blowing up on TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.320
every social media platform has wanted a piece&nbsp;
of the pie including YouTube who launched shorts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.520
in September of 2020. From a market share&nbsp;
perspective, this was very much a smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.080
move as TikTok was starting eat into YouTube’s&nbsp;
watch time and user engagement. But from a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:32.760
nuanced perspective, this may have actually been&nbsp;
a massive step backwards in terms of revenue and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.880
financials. And if you don’t believe me, take&nbsp;
it from Zuckerberg himself. Earlier this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.920
Zuckerberg confirmed that people watching&nbsp;
Instagram Reels instead of scrolling through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:49.160
main feed was costing the company $500 million&nbsp;
worth of revenue every single quarter. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.200
that’s just comparing the Instagram feed to&nbsp;
shorts. This revenue disparity is likely far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.160
larger when comparing full length videos with&nbsp;
shorts. To make things worse, it seems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:06.000
shorts are only growing in popularity. So,&nbsp;
is YouTube cannibalizing their own revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:18.423
with short form content, and what does mean&nbsp;
for the long term profitability of YouTube?

00:02:18.423 --> 00:02:20.640
