00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.320
for decades with hundreds of billions of dollars.&nbsp;
So, if you would like to do the same thing, please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:19.000
consider checking out Silo in the description&nbsp;
below. But anyway, you’re probably thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:25.200
there’s no way shorts revenue is that much&nbsp;
worse than long form revenue right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.960
I’ve got exact numbers and it’s bad. On&nbsp;
my channel, the average RPM for long form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:38.040
content is usually right around $5 meaning that&nbsp;
1000 views generates $5 worth of revenue. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:45.000
do you wanna guess what the RPM is for short form&nbsp;
content? Maybe a dollar? Maybe 50 cents? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:51.480
it's actually at 6 cents and that’s the highest&nbsp;
I’ve ever seen it. Usually, it’s at 5 cents. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:58.880
right off the bat, short form revenue per view&nbsp;
is about 100 times less but that’s not exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.840
a fair comparison because long form views&nbsp;
and short form views aren’t equal. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:10.720
ad revenue has less to do with views and more to&nbsp;
do with watch time. If you’re watching shorts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.000
you see an ad every 10 shorts, then each shorts&nbsp;
view is only worth 1/10 of an ad impression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.840
If you’re watching a 45 minute long Magnates&nbsp;
Media documentary, however, and there’s an ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:27.320
every 3 minutes, well then, each view could be&nbsp;
worth as much as 15 ad impressions. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:32.880
there’s more variables at play like average watch&nbsp;
time and topic and seasonality and all of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.680
but that’s the general idea. Shorts earn less&nbsp;
revenue because each shorts view is less valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.720
than each long form view. But, even when we&nbsp;
account for this, shorts end up with far less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:50.320
revenue per view. Let’s take my channel again for&nbsp;
example. With long form content, this channel gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:56.240
just over 6 minutes in average view duration. This&nbsp;
means that 1000 views translates to 6000 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.800
of watch time and $5 worth of ad revenue. In other&nbsp;
words, $1 worth of ad revenue is equivalent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.880
1200 minutes of watch time when it comes to long&nbsp;
form content. In terms of short form content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:12.720
the average short on this channel gets about&nbsp;
30 seconds worth of average view duration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.680
This means that 1000 shorts views translates to&nbsp;
500 minutes of watch time and $0.06 worth of ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:25.160
revenue. In other words, $1 worth of ad revenue is&nbsp;
equivalent to over 8000 minutes of watch time when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:31.000
it comes to short form content. That’s nearly&nbsp;
7 times less than long form content and if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.320
apply this math to the entirety of YouTube,&nbsp;
things become ugly pretty quickly. Every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.160
YouTube pulls in over a billion hours of watch&nbsp;
time. If all of this watch time was from long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:47.000
form content, YouTube would generate roughly $18&nbsp;
billion worth of ad revenue every single. If all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.520
of this watch time was from short form content,&nbsp;
however, YouTube would only generate about $2.6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:58.920
billion worth of ad revenue or over $15 billion&nbsp;
less. I think you can see how promoting shorts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:04.840
could have a massive impact on YouTube’s top line.&nbsp;
Zuckerberg realized this months ago and that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:10.280
Meta stopped paying bonuses to Reels creators.&nbsp;
It’s really quite counter productive if you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:17.840
about. Meta was literally paying creators to make&nbsp;
content that would make Meta far less money. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:23.000
no wonder they stopped but it’s not as easy&nbsp;
for YouTube. For instagram, their bread and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:29.000
butter is pictures so not emphasizing short&nbsp;
form videos isn’t a big deal. For YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.400
their bread and butter is videos, so not&nbsp;
emphasizing the new trend with videos is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:40.840
big deal. So, YouTube is caught in this awkward&nbsp;
catch 22 situation. They have to promote shorts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:47.280
to remain competitive and retain watch time&nbsp;
against TikTok but promoting shorts is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:59.400
directly leading to substantially less revenue for&nbsp;
the platform. So, what exactly is the solution?

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.480
THE SOLUTION?:
The reality is that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:08.320
is no simple solution this situation. So, YouTube&nbsp;
is simply trying their best to keep things moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.480
forward from three angles starting with doubling&nbsp;
down on long form content. When one part of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.680
business isn’t doing as well, one solution is to&nbsp;
double down on the part of your business that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:25.160
doing well and have this part of this business&nbsp;
make up for the other part. And it seems that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:31.240
is exactly what YouTube is doing with long form&nbsp;
content. I know a lot of you wont like this but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:36.720
YouTube is moving towards showing more ads on long&nbsp;
form content. The most recent of these changes is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.760
an update to ad control for creators. As you&nbsp;
may know, creators currently have the control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.480
to choose between pre-roll, post-roll, skippable,&nbsp;
and non skippable ads. But, starting in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:53.400
creators will only have the option to turn pre&nbsp;
roll and post roll ads on or off. And it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.520
be up to YouTube to decide whether its skippable&nbsp;
or non skippable and whether it appears before or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:04.000
after a video or both. Moving forward, YouTube&nbsp;
is also introducing a new option when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.320
to mid roll ads called manual plus automatic.&nbsp;
Essentially, this option allows creators to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:13.960
manually place mid roll ads throughout the video&nbsp;
and YouTube to show even more ads throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:19.360
video on top of that whenever they want. If&nbsp;
you ask YouTube about these policy changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.800
they’ll probably say something like “These&nbsp;
policies changes are meant to make the YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:29.440
ad experience more refined and enjoyable.”&nbsp;
But, that’s just code for “We’re taking more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.840
control over ads so that we can optimize their&nbsp;
placement and length to maximize revenue.” And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:39.320
this is just the most recent development in&nbsp;
YouTube’s monetization effort. YouTube has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.520
been rolling out such changes this entire year.&nbsp;
For example, they have become much more serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:50.200
when it comes to blocking ad block. They’ve been&nbsp;
experimenting with 30 second ads on YouTube TV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:55.880
And, they have even gone as far as stopping video&nbsp;
playback for users with AdBlock. So, YouTube is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:02.400
very much doubling down on long form monetization&nbsp;
but that’s only half the story. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.680
YouTube is trying their best to convince&nbsp;
advertisers to give short form content a try.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.600
One of the main reasons that shorts ad revenue&nbsp;
is so horrendous is because advertisers are quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.880
hesitant when it comes to short form advertising.&nbsp;
For one, a lot of them aren’t familiar with how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.920
to craft short form ads that convert but honestly,&nbsp;
that’s something that they can figure out by just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:31.880
hiring the right people. The larger issue is that&nbsp;
they view short form content as less than and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:37.920
no wonder why. Most short form content is cringey,&nbsp;
braindead, useless dumb entertainment that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:45.360
forgot in a matter of minutes. So, advertising&nbsp;
next to this sort of content isn’t exactly ideal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.680
but here’s the thing, social media ads in general&nbsp;
used to be viewed in the same light 10 to 15 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:55.960
ago and that’s why social media marketing was&nbsp;
way cheaper than traditional TV marketing. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:01.440
over time, some more progressive companies stepped&nbsp;
forward, experimented with social media marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:07.080
and showed the entire world just how effective&nbsp;
it could be. And today, many niches on YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:13.520
like finance and enterpreneurship demand the&nbsp;
same CPMs as prime time television. And YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.440
is hoping that with time, advertisers will&nbsp;
realize that its the same thing with short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:24.440
form content. But realistically speaking, it’ll&nbsp;
take several years for such a shift to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:30.240
and all of this still leaves YouTube vulnerable&nbsp;
to the next shift in video medium whether that AR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:40.800
or VR or whatever else. And that’s precisely why&nbsp;
YouTube is heavily leaning into strategy number 3.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.600
THE GREAT PIVOT:
Optimizing long form monetization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:52.400
and promoting short form monetization is great and&nbsp;
all but what YouTube really wants is independence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:57.840
from all of this. When YouTube first came out,&nbsp;
it was just a fun platform where people shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.000
simple videos that they made by themselves or with&nbsp;
their friends, and that still exists on one side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.880
of YouTube. But the more popular side of YouTube&nbsp;
is filled with the MrBeasts and Marques Brownlees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:12.960
and Good Mythical Mornings of the world and all&nbsp;
of these guys are just straight up production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.800
studios. Even creators who seem more relaxed&nbsp;
like Ryan Trahan aren’t. You know the saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:24.800
it takes effort to seem effortless. Well, that’s&nbsp;
exactly what’s going on there. And as all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:30.480
creators evolved so did the audience. Back in&nbsp;
the day, YouTube was something that would people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:36.200
would use to fill up free time. Today, YouTube&nbsp;
is something that hundreds of millions of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:42.440
use as their primary form of entertainment.&nbsp;
So, it’s not far fetched for YouTube to want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.800
all these people to directly pay for their use&nbsp;
of the platform which brings us into YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:53.320
Premium. YouTube currently has a total of 2&nbsp;
billion monthly active users out of which 80&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:58.520
million are already paid subscribers. So, right&nbsp;
off the bat, YouTube is doing pretty well when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:03.800
it comes to paid subscribers but it’s actually&nbsp;
even better than it looks. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.400
while it’s cool that YouTube reaches 2 billion&nbsp;
people every month, most of these people aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:14.400
really serious users. Most of them probably just&nbsp;
watch a video here and there and that’s why it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:20.720
far more important to look at daily active users.&nbsp;
YouTube currently stands at 122 million daily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:26.600
active users. Of course, not every single core&nbsp;
user is gonna use YouTube everyday single day. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:32.160
YouTube's core user base is likely around 200&nbsp;
to 300 million people. Given that 80 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:38.200
of these people are already paying, YouTube has&nbsp;
already converted 25 to 40% of their core user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:45.240
base into paid subscribers, so their primary goal&nbsp;
moving forward would be to convert the rest. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:50.960
however, is a bit of a tricky situation. YouTube&nbsp;
needs to make ads annoying enough such that all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:56.400
their core user base switches to premium but not&nbsp;
so annoying that they bleed watch time to TikTok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:02.600
and Meta. And this is what all of YouTube’s recent&nbsp;
moves have been all about: blocking ad block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:07.600
increasing ad length, and reducing creator&nbsp;
control over ads. It’s all about striking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:12.960
perfect balance to maximize YouTube premium users&nbsp;
while still keeping YouTube free and accessible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:17.880
casual viewers. This has been the play for a few&nbsp;
years now, but now that they’re starting to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:23.240
the pain of declining revenue, the pressure is&nbsp;
on as they go all in on trying to free themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:29.360
from their dependency on ad revenue. If you’d like&nbsp;
to free yourself from your dependency on your job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:34.280
by investing for the long term, please consider&nbsp;
checking out Silo in the description below.

